-- title:
--   A lattice point belongs to its own block
-- statement:
--   Let
--   $$\mathbb{Z}^d \;=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^d \;:\; x_i \in \mathbb{Z} \text{ for every } i \,\} \qquad (\texttt{lattice d})$$
--   be the standard integer lattice, write $\|x\|_\infty = \max_i |x_i|$ for the maximum norm, and let
--   $$\bar A_\ell(x) \;=\; \{\, y \in \mathbb{R}^d : \|y - x\|_\infty \le \ell/2 \,\}$$
--   be the closed cube of side $\ell$ centred at $x$. The **block** of size $\ell$ based at $x$ is the set of lattice points inside that cube,
--   $$Q_\ell(x) \;=\; \mathbb{Z}^d \cap \bar A_\ell(x) \;=\; \{\, y \in \mathbb{Z}^d : \|y - x\|_\infty \le \ell/2 \,\} \qquad (\texttt{block } \ell\ x).$$
--   The claim is: if $\ell \ge 0$ and $x \in \mathbb{Z}^d$, then
--   $$x \in Q_\ell(x).$$
--
--   This is the reflexivity of the block construction. It is what guarantees that the blocks based at lattice points cover the lattice, so that every site has a block to be renormalized into; small as it is, it is the fact that makes the renormalized structure exhaustive, and it is used whenever one passes from a site to a block containing it.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The hypothesis $\ell \ge 0$ is the weakest possible: the degenerate value $\ell = 0$ is allowed, in which case $Q_0(x) = \{x\}$ and the assertion still holds. Membership in the lattice is required, so nothing is asserted for a non-lattice base point.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Renormalization.lean#L147-L154

